When trustd cannot be reached, leave a coherent trust result and a real chain.

THE FALLBACK ALREADY LIES DELIBERATELY. SecTrustEvaluateIfNecessary has a branch for "we failed to
talk to securityd", written for a ramdisk case, which returns SUCCESS after setting only the leaf
certificate -- in Apple's own words, to make it seem like we did a cert evaluation. What it does NOT
do is set a trust result, so every caller is handed success together with kSecTrustResultInvalid,
which the header defines as SecTrustEvaluate has not yet been called, and each one mishandles it
differently: errSecTimestampNotTrusted in the timestamp path, -50 in validateDirectory, and an
assert(false) marked should never happen in tsaSupport.

In this container that branch is not a fallback, it is the ONLY path, and the effect is that NO
third-party bundle can validate its own signature. Measured on three in one container: MoneyMoney,
iTerm and LibreOffice all returned -67061.

TWO CHANGES, both on that one branch, both reversible with CIDER_TRUST_STRICT=1.

Report kSecTrustResultUnspecified rather than leaving Invalid. This IS a divergence and it gives
something up: we reach here only when trustd could not be talked to at all, so nothing was verified,
and Unspecified says no chain objection about an evaluation that did not happen. That is the same
thing the surrounding code already decided when it chose to return true.

Build the chain from ALL the certificates the caller supplied, not just the leaf. Apple keeps one
element for an ASR case that only wants the leaf public key; a one element chain is not a chain, and
code signing then asks whether it is an Apple developer certificate, which needs the issuer above
the leaf, and fails with -50 well away from the cause. These are the certs handed in with the
signature, claiming no more than that.

AND ONE FIX IN CODESIGNING: isAppleDeveloperCert must not be able to fail a validation by throwing.
It parses a fixed requirement string and evaluates it against the chain, and here it throws -50 every
time, which reaches the caller as this bundle is invalid. It is a CONSISTENCY CHECK, not the
signature check: its only use is to decide whether to cross check the team identifier. Now reported
and treated as not-a-developer-certificate. A bad signature still fails, from the CMS layer above,
untouched.

MEASURED, the verdict walking down the chain as each cause was removed:
    -67061  a false label, the timestamp discarding a good signature      (patch 0007)
    -50     trust evaluation never happened
    100022  errSecErrnoBase + EINVAL, from fcntl(F_NOCACHE)               (xnu patch 0020)
    -67055  errSecCSResourcesInvalid, the sealed resource directory
and verifySignature now returns normally, validateExecutable PASSES, and staticValidate RETURNS for
the first time. MoneyMoney still shows its damaged-file alert, said plainly: -67055 is still a
failure and the sealed resource directory is the next piece of work.
--- a/OSX/sec/Security/SecTrust.c
+++ b/OSX/sec/Security/SecTrust.c
@@ -1687,11 +1687,51 @@
                      _chain and return success with a failure as the trustResult, to
                      make it seem like we did a cert evaluation, so ASR can extract
                      the public key from the leaf. */
-                    SecCertificateRef leafCert = (SecCertificateRef)CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(trust->_certificates, 0);
-                    CFArrayRef leafCertArray = CFArrayCreate(NULL, (const void**)&leafCert, 1, &kCFTypeArrayCallBacks);
-                    trust->_chain = leafCertArray;
+                    /*
+                     * ALL THE CERTIFICATES THE CALLER SUPPLIED, not just the leaf.
+                     *
+                     * Apple builds a one-element chain here on purpose, for an ASR case that only
+                     * wants the leaf's public key. In this container this is not a fallback but the
+                     * ONLY path, and a one-element chain is not a chain: code signing then asks
+                     * whether it is an Apple developer certificate, which needs the issuer above the
+                     * leaf, and the evaluation fails with -50 well away from the cause.
+                     *
+                     * These are the certs the caller handed in with the signature -- claiming no more
+                     * than that. CIDER_TRUST_STRICT=1 restores the leaf-only behaviour.
+                     */
+                    const char *cider_strict = getenv("CIDER_TRUST_STRICT");
+                    bool cider_lenient = (cider_strict == NULL || cider_strict[0] == '\0');
+
+                    if (cider_lenient) {
+                        trust->_chain = CFArrayCreateCopy(NULL, trust->_certificates);
+                    } else {
+                        SecCertificateRef leafCert = (SecCertificateRef)CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(trust->_certificates, 0);
+                        trust->_chain = CFArrayCreate(NULL, (const void**)&leafCert, 1, &kCFTypeArrayCallBacks);
+                    }
                     if (error)
                         CFReleaseNull(*error);
+                    /*
+                     * LEAVE A COHERENT RESULT, NOT kSecTrustResultInvalid.
+                     *
+                     * This branch already returns SUCCESS deliberately -- Apple's own comment says it
+                     * does so "to make it seem like we did a cert evaluation". What it does not do is
+                     * set a trust result, so every caller is handed success plus a value the header
+                     * defines as "SecTrustEvaluate has not yet been called", and each mishandles it
+                     * differently: errSecTimestampNotTrusted in the timestamp path, -50 in
+                     * validateDirectory, an assert(false) marked "should never happen" in tsaSupport.
+                     * The net effect in this container is that NO third-party bundle can validate its
+                     * own signature: MoneyMoney, iTerm and LibreOffice all returned -67061.
+                     *
+                     * THIS IS A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE AND IT GIVES SOMETHING UP. We reach here only
+                     * when trustd could not be talked to at all, so nothing was verified; reporting
+                     * Unspecified says "no chain objection" about an evaluation that did not happen.
+                     * That is the same thing the surrounding code already decided when it chose to
+                     * return true. CIDER_TRUST_STRICT=1 restores the old behaviour, and with it the
+                     * damaged-file alert.
+                     */
+                    if (cider_lenient) {
+                        trust->_trustResult = kSecTrustResultUnspecified;
+                    }
                     return true;
                 }
                 return trust->_trustResult != kSecTrustResultInvalid;
